.TH MD_FLUSH 3 "2026-10-16" "libmorsedec 0.2.0" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
md_flush \- drain pending decoding and seal the stream
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <morsedec.h>

int md_flush(morsedec_decoder_t *decoder);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
Drain pending decoding and seal the stream.
.SH FUNCTION PARAMETERS
.TP
.I decoder
Decoder handle. Calls using this handle must be serialized, including queries.
.SH RETURN VALUE
Returns MORSEDEC_SUCCESS (1) on success, or MORSEDEC_FAILURE (0) and sets
errno on failure. Do not inspect errno after success.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EINVAL
decoder is NULL.
.SH REMARKS
Pads a partial sample hop, feeds approximately one second of silence through
the pipeline, and commits pending output. External-spectrum mode instead
receives zero-power frames. Query final snapshots after return. Repeated calls
succeed without adding more frames or output. The frame count includes
synthetic drain frames, so derived times can exceed the actual input duration.
.PP
All processing calls fail after flush, including zero-count sample calls. Call
md_reset() before feeding another stream. Frequency pin/unpin operations also
fail on a flushed handle. Queries remain available. Flushing does not delete
the handle.
.SH THREAD SAFETY
Serialize all calls that access the same handle, including getters. Different
handles may process concurrently after construction. These functions are not
specified as async-signal-safe.
.SH VERSION
This md_ entry point is available since libmorsedec 0.2.0 (shared-library ABI
1).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR libmorsedec (3),
.BR md_get_characters (3),
.BR md_get_text (3),
.BR md_get_frame_count (3),
.BR md_reset (3),
.BR md_delete (3)
